= u.id WHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\n\n## Scans the query forces\n\n1. **Full scan of `users` (non-sargable predicate).** `lower(u.email)` wraps the column in a function, so a plain index on `email` can't be used. The planner has to read every row, compute `lower()`, and compare. This is the worst problem here, because it hits before the join and grows with table size.\n2. **Scan of `orders` on the join.** The join key is `o.user_id`. If there's no index on `orders.user_id` (many schemas don't add one automatically, since FK constraints don't always create it), the planner has to scan `orders` using a hash join or, worse, a nested loop that rescans it. Either way it reads all of `orders` to find one user's rows.\n3. **Wide row materialization from `SELECT *`.** It pulls every column from both tables. That defeats index-only scans, and it duplicates the user's columns on every order row. It also returns duplicate column names (`id`, `created_at`, and so on), which many clients handle badly.\n\nI haven't seen the schema or `EXPLAIN` output. Scans 1 and 2 are what the query forces if those indexes are missing. Check them against the real plan.\n\n## Indexes and rewrites\n\n**Fix the lookup on `users`.** Pick one:\n\n- **Expression index**, which keeps the query as written:\n  ```sql\n  CREATE INDEX idx_users_email_lower ON users (lower(email));\n  ```\n- **Normalize the data and drop the function.** Store emails lowercased, or use a case-insensitive type (`citext` in Postgres, or a `_ci` collation in MySQL). Then query `WHERE u.email = 'a@example.org'` against a unique index on `email`. I'd choose this if you control writes, because it also enforces uniqueness.\n\n**Index the join side:**\n```sql\nCREATE INDEX idx_orders_user_id ON orders (user_id);\n```\nIf you usually sort orders by recency, use `(user_id, created_at DESC)` instead. That avoids a sort.\n\n**Rewrite the select list:**\n```sql\nSELECT o.id, o.created_at, o.total, u.id AS user_id, u.email\nFROM users u\nJOIN orders o ON o.user_id = u.id\nWHERE lower(u.email) = 'a@example.org';\n```\nList only the columns you need. If the list is small enough, a covering index on `orders (user_id) INCLUDE (...)` can make it index-only. Putting `users` first is only a readability change. The planner picks the join order itself.\n\n**Expected plan after the fixes:** an index seek on `users` returning one row, then an index seek on `orders` by `user_id`. Cost then scales with that user's order count, not with table size.\n\n## Things to check\n- Run `EXPLAIN (ANALYZE, BUFFERS)` before and after to confirm the planner uses the new indexes.\n- If the user can have many orders, add `LIMIT` or pagination.\n- Make sure the app lowercases the input the same way the index does (same locale and collation).", "commits": [], "branch": "main", "before": {}, "after": {}}}